From d03bc8a1d4735e58fcd7a6087fea0cbbc2428aa6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 17:26:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 040/206] fix(shorebird): metadata is a constant displacement,
 and back out the dead-strip hook

Two corrections to 094fc0de070, both coming out of the disassembly of their
Sim->CPU shared body (spikes WRAPPER_STUBS.md, route-a-research 1838d3d).

**MetadataForWrapper was wrong.** Their body reaches a wrapper's metadata in
three instructions -- mov x17,#0x8000 / add x16,x16,x17 / ldr x16,[x16] -- with
no mask and no index arithmetic. It works because landing_pad_size ==
metadata_size == 8, so a single constant displacement maps pad i onto
Metadata[i]. Ours recovered the page by masking and indexed from the mapping's
RW boundary, which differs from theirs by exactly
offset_of_first_wrapper_in_page_: their metadata array is shifted along with the
pads. Both versions are internally consistent, which is why every existing test
passed either way. Restoring the old formula now produces 4088 errors in the
round-trip test, so the corrected tests do distinguish them.

This also disproves the reason I gave for why their MetadataForWrapper takes no
page argument. It is not that MappingAlignment() makes the page recoverable by
masking; it is that the page is not needed at all. MappingStart survives for
ContainsEntryPoint's ownership test, which is a real use. Three comments that
asserted the old rationale are corrected rather than deleted.

Added a test that the last pad's metadata still lands inside the mapping. It
only just does -- offset + 49112 + 8 against 65536 -- so the scheme depends on
offset staying below one page. That held implicitly; now it is pinned.

**Backed out InitSimToCpuWrapperPage and DART_NOINLINE.** That was a no-op
function called from StubCode::Init() for no purpose other than to keep symbols
alive under -dead_strip, and it is not in their API: the name appears 0 times in
their binary against 34 WrapperAllocator symbols. It also did not work.
wrapper_symbols_check.sh reported 0 -> 2 and passed, but both symbols were one
config accessor listed twice (a FUN debug entry plus its text symbol) while
every WrapperAllocator symbol was still stripped.

So the script was the faulty instrument: its pattern also accepted
CPUToSimConfig|SimToCPUConfig, which a config getter satisfies on its own. It
now matches WrapperAllocator and nothing else, and it fails -- honestly -- with
0. It should keep failing until a caller exists on the CPU<->Sim transition path
that actually wraps an entry point.

Kept from that commit: SimToCPUConfig::GetStubCodeRange reporting the real stub
with a StubCode::HasBeenInitialized() guard. That part is correct and is theirs.

59/59 shorebird tests pass. WrapperAllocator symbols in product gen_snapshot: 0,
unchanged and correctly reported as a failure.

